Here’s a simple Java program that checks whether a given string is a valid email address using a regular expression.

```java
public class EmailValidator {

    // Simple but reasonably strict email regex
    private static final String EMAIL_REGEX =
            "^[A-Za-z0-9+_.-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\\.[A-Za-z]{2,}$";

    public static boolean isValidEmail(String email) {
        if (email == null || email.isEmpty()) {
            return false;
        }
        return email.matches(EMAIL_REGEX);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] testEmails = {
                "user@example.com",
                "user.name@example.co.uk",
                "invalid.email",
                "user@domain",
                "user@domain.c",
                "user+tag@example.com",
                "user..name@example.com",
                null,
                ""
        };

        for (String email : testEmails) {
            System.out.println(email + " -> " + isValidEmail(email));
        }
    }
}
```

How it works:
- `EMAIL_REGEX` enforces:
  - Local part before `@`: letters, digits, `+`, `.`, `_`, `-`
  - Domain: letters, digits, `.` and `-`
  - Top-level domain: at least 2 letters
- `matches()` checks if the entire string conforms to the pattern.
- `main()` demonstrates usage with several test cases.

